Z||000000||Patrick's Dad||12-17-2000||09:17 PM||||My boy is only 4½ years old, so he's kinda stumped, too....<P>My computer reads a nonexistant CD-ROM Drive, D:/ If I place a CD into my actual CD-ROM Drive, it shows up on CD-ROM Drive E:/ All other things seem to be normal, including FDD and Zip.<P>I think that this is why my computer is often sluggish, as it's trying to read a drive that isn't there.... How do I delete a drive that my computer thinks is there, buthas no driver, and no physical presence?<P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Oreif||12-17-2000||10:22 PM||||Select:<BR>Start<BR>Settings<BR>Control Panel<BR>System<BR>Device Manager<BR>Click on the "+" by CDROM.<BR>If it shows 2 of them go to each one and select "Properties" and see which one is drive "D". Delete that one than go to the other one and assign it to what you want (E or D) Make sure you set it in both boxes (Starting letter and stopping letter)<BR>Then reboot and see what happens.<P>One word of caution: If you have loaded programs that require the CD to run, You need to keep the drive letter the same. If your CDROM has always been "E" then leave it at "E". This could be how the computer added "D" A program that went looking for "D"<BR>May have added it for you.<P>"We Are Microsoft....Resistance is Futile!"<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 12-17-2000).]||||reg||1
